ground_truth:
PHP cannot directly detect a browser button click because it runs on the server. In JavaScript, model each Ajax view as a browser-history entry and listen for history navigation.

### Modern approach: History API

```html
<script>
function showView(url, addToHistory = true) {
    fetch(url, { headers: { "X-Requested-With": "XMLHttpRequest" } })
        .then(response => response.text())
        .then(html => {
            document.getElementById("content").innerHTML = html;

            if (addToHistory) {
                history.pushState({ url: url }, "", url);
            }
        });
}

// Give the initial page a state.
history.replaceState({ url: location.href }, "", location.href);

// Called when Back or Forward activates another history entry.
window.addEventListener("popstate", function (event) {
    const url = event.state?.url || location.href;
    showView(url, false); // Do not create another history entry.
});
</script>
```

When handling an Ajax link:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("click", function (event) {
    const link = event.target.closest("a.ajax-link");
    if (!link) return;

    event.preventDefault();
    showView(link.href);
});
```

`popstate` indicates navigation to another history entry; it can result from Back, Forward, or programmatic history traversal, not exclusively a physical Back-button click.

### Hash-based alternative

For older applications, store the current view in the URL fragment:

```javascript
window.addEventListener("hashchange", function () {
    const view = location.hash.slice(1);
    loadView(view);
});

function navigateTo(view) {
    location.hash = encodeURIComponent(view);
}
```

Changing the hash creates history entries, and `hashchange` runs when the user goes back or forward. Very old browsers without `hashchange` require polling `location.hash` periodically.

The URLs represented in history should ideally also work when loaded or refreshed directly, with PHP rendering the full page or an appropriate fallback.

Final answer: Use JavaScript’s History API (`pushState`/`replaceState` plus `popstate`), or `location.hash` plus `hashchange`; PHP alone cannot detect a browser Back-button click.

