There is no standard API for enumerating and selectively cancelling requests initiated implicitly by DOM elements such as `<img>`, `<script>`, or `<link>`.

To stop all resource loading in the current browsing context, use:

```js
window.stop();
```

This is roughly equivalent to pressing the browser’s Stop button. It is broad: it may stop relevant loads as well as irrelevant ones, and it is not a substitute for aborting requests that you explicitly created.

For selective cancellation, you must retain a cancellable handle:

```js
const controller = new AbortController();

fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });

// Later:
controller.abort();
```

For XHR, retain the object and call `xhr.abort()`.

Implicit DOM requests generally have no reliable cancellation handle. Removing an element or changing its `src` may cause some browsers to abandon the load, but this is not a portable guarantee. If cancellation is important, load the resource yourself with `fetch()` and an `AbortController`, then assign a resulting blob URL where appropriate:

```js
const controller = new AbortController();

const response = await fetch(imageUrl, { signal: controller.signal });
const blob = await response.blob();
const objectUrl = URL.createObjectURL(blob);

img.src = objectUrl;

// If no longer needed before fetch completes:
controller.abort();
```

Also revoke blob URLs when finished:

```js
URL.revokeObjectURL(objectUrl);
```

The practical solution is therefore to track and abort explicit `fetch`/XHR operations, avoid creating unneeded resource elements through lazy loading, and reserve `window.stop()` for the all-or-nothing case.

Final answer: Use `window.stop()` to stop all page resource loading; selective cancellation of already-running implicit DOM resource requests is not generally supported, so use tracked `fetch`/XHR requests with `AbortController`/`abort()` when selective cancellation is required.

