The program below tokenizes the text, applies Unicode-aware case folding, constructs every consecutive three-word sequence, and reports those occurring more than once. By default, each trigram must contain three different lexemes.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl

use strict;
use warnings;
use feature 'fc';
use open qw(:std :encoding(UTF-8));

# Set to 0 if repeated words within a trigram, such as
# "as sweet as", should also be accepted.
my $require_three_distinct_lexemes = 1;

# Read from files named on the command line, or from standard input.
my $text = do {
    local $/;
    <>;
};

# Keep apostrophes inside words and normalize case.
my @words = map { fc($_) }
            ($text =~ /\p{L}+(?:['’]\p{L}+)*/gu);

my (%frequency, %phrase);
my @order;

for my $i (0 .. $#words - 2) {
    my @trigram = @words[$i .. $i + 2];

    if ($require_three_distinct_lexemes) {
        my %distinct = map { $_ => 1 } @trigram;
        next unless keys(%distinct) == 3;
    }

    # The separator cannot occur in tokens produced by the regex.
    my $key = join "\x1F", @trigram;

    unless (exists $frequency{$key}) {
        push @order, $key;
        $phrase{$key} = join ' ', @trigram;
    }

    ++$frequency{$key};
}

for my $key (@order) {
    next unless $frequency{$key} > 1;
    printf "%-25s %d occurrences\n",
        $phrase{$key}, $frequency{$key};
}
```

Example use:

```bash
perl recurring_trigrams.pl <<'TEXT'
I adore apples but not as much as I adore apricots because apricots are healthier than apples but not as sweet as apples. I suppose it's a good thing that apricots aren't as sweet as apples because then I'd probably like apricots more than apples.
TEXT
```

