prompt:
If I have the following code:

interface A {
    foo: string
}

interface B extends A {
    foo: "bar"
}

interface C extends A {
    foo: "baz"
}

interface D ...


namely, one interface and a number of other interfaces that extend the original, but fix a specific property of the original to a single string literal, is it possible for me to design a type guard for interfaces that extend the original by comparing against this property in a generic way? Something like the following:

function isExtensionOfA<T extends A>(obj: A): obj is T {
    return (obj.foo === T.foo.literal_value)
}

ground_truth:
No. Type parameters and interfaces are erased at runtime, so `T.foo.literal_value` does not exist. It would also be unsafe because callers can choose any `T extends A`, including one with additional required properties.

Pass the expected literal value as a runtime argument instead:

```ts
interface A {
    foo: string;
}

interface B extends A {
    foo: "bar";
}

interface C extends A {
    foo: "baz";
}

function hasFoo<K extends string>(
    obj: A,
    value: K
): obj is A & { foo: K } {
    return obj.foo === value;
}

declare const obj: A;

if (hasFoo(obj, "bar")) {
    // obj is A & { foo: "bar" }, structurally equivalent to B here
}
```

For a fixed set of extensions, you can explicitly model the discriminated union:

```ts
type Extension = B | C;

function isExtension<K extends Extension["foo"]>(
    obj: A,
    value: K
): obj is Extract<Extension, { foo: K }> {
    return obj.foo === value;
}

if (isExtension(obj, "bar")) {
    // obj is B
} else if (isExtension(obj, "baz")) {
    // obj is C
}
```

This is sound when `foo` fully identifies each extension. If, for example, `B` also had required properties other than `foo`, those properties would need runtime validation too; comparing only `foo` would not prove that the object is a `B`.

Final answer: A generic type parameter cannot be inspected at runtime; pass the literal discriminator as a value and narrow to `A & { foo: K }`, or use a known discriminated union with `Extract`.

