## JavaScript’s `reduce`: more than summing numbers

`Array.prototype.reduce()` processes an array from left to right while carrying an accumulated value from one element to the next.

```js
const result = array.reduce(
  (accumulator, currentValue, index, array) => {
    return updatedAccumulator;
  },
  initialValue
);
```

The accumulator can be anything: a number, string, array, object, `Map`, function, promise, or domain-specific state.

### Basic aggregation

```js
const total = [10, 20, 30].reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0);
// 60
```

Supplying an initial value is generally best practice. Without one, the first present element becomes the initial accumulator:

```js
[10, 20, 30].reduce((sum, value) => sum + value); // 60
[].reduce((sum, value) => sum + value); // TypeError
```

An explicit initial value also prevents subtle type errors:

```js
const values = ["2", 3, 4];

values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + Number(value), 0); // 9
```

## Useful and lesser-known applications

### 1. Counting frequencies

```js
const words = ["red", "blue", "red", "green", "blue", "red"];

const counts = words.reduce((result, word) => {
  result[word] = (result[word] ?? 0) + 1;
  return result;
}, Object.create(null));

// { red: 3, blue: 2, green: 1 }
```

For arbitrary or untrusted keys, a `Map` is safer and more expressive:

```js
const counts = words.reduce((map, word) => {
  map.set(word, (map.get(word) ?? 0) + 1);
  return map;
}, new Map());
```

### 2. Grouping records

```js
const people = [
  { name: "Ana", department: "Engineering" },
  { name: "Ben", department: "Sales" },
  { name: "Cara", department: "Engineering" }
];

const byDepartment = people.reduce((groups, person) => {
  (groups[person.department] ??= []).push(person);
  return groups;
}, Object.create(null));
```

Modern JavaScript also provides `Object.groupBy()` and `Map.groupBy()` in supported environments, so `reduce()` is most useful when grouping must be combined with additional calculations.

### 3. Creating an index for fast lookup

Repeatedly searching an array is linear. A reducer can convert records into a lookup table:

```js
const users = [
  { id: 12, name: "Ari" },
  { id: 27, name: "Bo" }
];

const usersById = users.reduce((index, user) => {
  index.set(user.id, user);
  return index;
}, new Map());

usersById.get(27); // { id: 27, name: "Bo" }
```

### 4. Partitioning in one pass

Instead of filtering twice:

```js
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

const [evens, odds] = numbers.reduce(
  ([evens, odds], number) => {
    (number % 2 === 0 ? evens : odds).push(number);
    return [evens, odds];
  },
  [[], []]
);
```

A version that avoids recreating the outer array on every iteration is:

```js
const [evens, odds] = numbers.reduce(
  (parts, number) => {
    parts[number % 2 === 0 ? 0 : 1].push(number);
    return parts;
  },
  [[], []]
);
```

### 5. Deduplicating by a property

`Set` works for primitive values, but `reduce()` can deduplicate objects using a key:

```js
const records = [
  { id: 1, value: "old" },
  { id: 2, value: "two" },
  { id: 1, value: "new" }
];

const uniqueById = [
  ...records.reduce((map, record) => {
    map.set(record.id, record); // Later records replace earlier ones
    return map;
  }, new Map()).values()
];
```

### 6. Flattening and transforming simultaneously

```js
const departments = [
  { employees: ["Ana", "Ben"] },
  { employees: ["Cara"] }
];

const names = departments.reduce((result, department) => {
  result.push(...department.employees.map(name => name.toUpperCase()));
  return result;
}, []);

// ["ANA", "BEN", "CARA"]
```

For ordinary flattening, `array.flat()` is usually clearer.

### 7. Computing several statistics in one traversal

```js
const values = [4, 8, 2, 10];

const stats = values.reduce(
  (result, value) => {
    result.count++;
    result.sum += value;
    result.min = Math.min(result.min, value);
    result.max = Math.max(result.max, value);
    return result;
  },
  {
    count: 0,
    sum: 0,
    min: Infinity,
    max: -Infinity
  }
);

stats.average = stats.count === 0 ? undefined : stats.sum / stats.count;
```

This can be more efficient than separate calls to `min`, `max`, and summation operations.

### 8. Building function pipelines

Because the accumulator need not be data, `reduce()` can combine functions:

```js
const pipe =
  (...functions) =>
  input =>
    functions.reduce((value, fn) => fn(value), input);

const normalize = pipe(
  text => text.trim(),
  text => text.toLowerCase(),
  text => text.replace(/\s+/g, "-")
);

normalize("  Hello   World  "); // "hello-world"
```

Right-to-left composition can use `reduceRight()`:

```js
const compose =
  (...functions) =>
  input =>
    functions.reduceRight((value, fn) => fn(value), input);
```

### 9. Promise sequencing

A promise can serve as the accumulator, allowing asynchronous operations to run serially:

```js
const tasks = [
  () => fetch("/step-1"),
  () => fetch("/step-2"),
  () => fetch("/step-3")
];

await tasks.reduce(
  (previous, task) => previous.then(() => task()),
  Promise.resolve()
);
```

To retain every result:

```js
const results = await tasks.reduce(
  async (previous, task) => {
    const collected = await previous;
    collected.push(await task());
    return collected;
  },
  Promise.resolve([])
);
```

This deliberately runs tasks one at a time. For independent operations that should run concurrently, prefer:

```js
const results = await Promise.all(tasks.map(task => task()));
```

A conventional `for...of` loop is often easier to understand for asynchronous sequencing.

### 10. Implementing a small state machine

The accumulator can represent evolving application state:

```js
const events = [
  { type: "deposit", amount: 100 },
  { type: "withdraw", amount: 30 },
  { type: "deposit", amount: 15 }
];

const account = events.reduce(
  (state, event) => {
    switch (event.type) {
      case "deposit":
        return { ...state, balance: state.balance + event.amount };
      case "withdraw":
        return { ...state, balance: state.balance - event.amount };
      default:
        return state;
    }
  },
  { balance: 0 }
);

// { balance: 85 }
```

This event-folding model is relevant to reducers in Redux, event sourcing, parsers, and replayable systems.

### 11. Constructing nested structures

A reducer can walk a path and create missing levels:

```js
function setPath(target, path, value) {
  path.slice(0, -1).reduce((current, key) => {
    return (current[key] ??= {});
  }, target)[path.at(-1)] = value;

  return target;
}

setPath({}, ["user", "preferences", "theme"], "dark");
// { user: { preferences: { theme: "dark" } } }
```

Production implementations should reject dangerous untrusted keys such as `__proto__`, `prototype`, and `constructor`, or use data structures that cannot cause prototype pollution.

### 12. Reproducing other array operations

Many array operations can be expressed as reductions:

```js
const mapped = [1, 2, 3].reduce((result, value) => {
  result.push(value * 2);
  return result;
}, []);

const filtered = [1, 2, 3, 4].reduce((result, value) => {
  if (value % 2 === 0) result.push(value);
  return result;
}, []);
```

This illustrates the generality of `reduce()`, but built-in `map()` and `filter()` are usually clearer when only one standard operation is needed.

## Important behavior and pitfalls

- **Sparse slots are skipped.** Like many array methods, `reduce()` does not invoke its callback for holes in sparse arrays.
- **Empty arrays need an initial value.** Otherwise, `reduce()` throws a `TypeError`.
- **The input is not automatically mutated.** Mutation occurs only if the callback explicitly modifies it or mutates referenced objects.
- **Array length is determined before traversal.** Elements appended beyond the initial length are not visited.
- **Repeated immutable copying may be expensive.** Code such as `{ ...acc, [key]: value }` copies the growing accumulator on every iteration and can become quadratic. Mutating a newly created local accumulator is often appropriate.
- **An async callback does not make `reduce()` automatically await values.** The accumulator must explicitly be a promise, and loops may be clearer.
- **Readability matters.** If the reducer requires intricate control flow or many side effects, a `for...of` loop may communicate intent better.

`reduce()` is most valuable when a sequence genuinely needs to be folded into one evolving result—especially for indexing, grouping, combined statistics, function composition, sequential promises, and state transitions—rather than as a universal replacement for clearer methods such as `map()`, `filter()`, `flat()`, or ordinary loops.

Final answer: JavaScript’s `reduce()` is a general-purpose folding operation that transforms a sequence into any accumulated value, with particularly useful applications in aggregation, grouping, indexing, partitioning, composition, asynchronous sequencing, and state-machine processing.

An efficient LRU cache combines:

- A `HashMap` for average \(O(1)\) key lookup.
- A doubly linked list to track usage order.
- The most recently accessed item at the front.
- The least recently used item at the back, allowing \(O(1)\) eviction.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Objects;

public final class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;

    // Sentinel nodes simplify insertion and removal.
    private final Node<K, V> head;
    private final Node<K, V> tail;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Capacity must be greater than zero");
        }

        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>(capacity);
        this.head = new Node<>(null, null);
        this.tail = new Node<>(null, null);

        head.next = tail;
        tail.previous = head;
    }

    /**
     * Returns the cached value, or null if the key is not present.
     * Accessing an entry makes it the most recently used entry.
     */
    public V get(K key) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(key, "Key cannot be null");

        Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
        if (node == null) {
            return null;
        }

        moveToFront(node);
        return node.value;
    }

    /**
     * Adds or updates an entry. If the capacity is exceeded,
     * the least recently used entry is removed.
     */
    public void put(K key, V value) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(key, "Key cannot be null");
        Objects.requireNonNull(value, "Value cannot be null");

        Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);

        if (existing != null) {
            existing.value = value;
            moveToFront(existing);
            return;
        }

        Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
        cache.put(key, newNode);
        addToFront(newNode);

        if (cache.size() > capacity) {
            Node<K, V> leastRecentlyUsed = tail.previous;
            removeNode(leastRecentlyUsed);
            cache.remove(leastRecentlyUsed.key);
        }
    }

    public boolean containsKey(K key) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(key, "Key cannot be null");
        return cache.containsKey(key);
    }

    public V remove(K key) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(key, "Key cannot be null");

        Node<K, V> node = cache.remove(key);
        if (node == null) {
            return null;
        }

        removeNode(node);
        return node.value;
    }

    public int size() {
        return cache.size();
    }

    public void clear() {
        cache.clear();
        head.next = tail;
        tail.previous = head;
    }

    private void moveToFront(Node<K, V> node) {
        removeNode(node);
        addToFront(node);
    }

    private void addToFront(Node<K, V> node) {
        node.previous = head;
        node.next = head.next;

        head.next.previous = node;
        head.next = node;
    }

    private void removeNode(Node<K, V> node) {
        node.previous.next = node.next;
        node.next.previous = node.previous;

        node.previous = null;
        node.next = null;
    }

    private static final class Node<K, V> {
        private final K key;
        private V value;
        private Node<K, V> previous;
        private Node<K, V> next;

        private Node(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }
}
```

Example usage:

```java
LRUCache<Integer, String> cache = new LRUCache<>(2);

cache.put(1, "one");
cache.put(2, "two");
cache.get(1);          // Key 1 becomes most recently used
cache.put(3, "three"); // Key 2 is evicted

System.out.println(cache.get(2)); // null
System.out.println(cache.get(1)); // one
System.out.println(cache.get(3)); // three
```

Both `get` and `put` run in average \(O(1)\) time, while memory usage is \(O(\text{capacity})\). This implementation is not thread-safe; concurrent use requires external synchronization or locking.

Final answer: Use a HashMap combined with a doubly linked list to provide O(1) average lookup, update, and least-recently-used eviction.

Below is a reusable custom validator that verifies that the password and confirmation password fields match.

### `password-match.validator.ts`

```ts
import {
  AbstractControl,
  ValidationErrors,
  ValidatorFn
} from '@angular/forms';

export function passwordMatchValidator(
  passwordField = 'password',
  confirmPasswordField = 'confirmPassword'
): ValidatorFn {
  return (form: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null => {
    const password = form.get(passwordField);
    const confirmPassword = form.get(confirmPasswordField);

    if (!password || !confirmPassword) {
      return null;
    }

    return password.value === confirmPassword.value
      ? null
      : { passwordMismatch: true };
  };
}
```

