JavaScript has no standard synchronous `sleep()` that blocks execution. Use `setTimeout()` to schedule code after a delay:

```javascript
console.log("First");

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("Runs after 1 second");
}, 1000);

console.log("Runs immediately");
```

For sequential-looking asynchronous code, create a Promise-based delay:

```javascript
const delay = ms => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

async function run() {
  console.log("First");
  await delay(1000);
  console.log("Runs after 1 second");
}

run();
```

Avoid busy-wait loops because they block the event loop and freeze the page. Also, jQuery’s `.delay()` only delays items in jQuery queues—such as animations—not arbitrary script execution.

Final answer: Use `setTimeout()` for delayed execution, or a Promise-based `delay()` with `await`; JavaScript has no general-purpose synchronous `wait()` method.

