```perl
use strict;
use warnings;

sub five_div_seq {
    my ($n) = @_;

    my $digit = '5';
    my @seq;

    # Match Python's min($n, 1000).
    my $start = $n < 1000 ? $n : 1000;

    # Examine numbers from $start down to, but not including, 100.
    # Only every 18th number is examined, matching Python's range(..., -18).
    for (my $num = $start; $num > 100; $num -= 18) {

        # Keep the number if it is divisible by either 9 or 14.
        if ($num % 9 == 0 || $num % 14 == 0) {

            # Also require the decimal representation to contain the digit 5.
            if (index("$num", $digit) != -1) {
                push @seq, $num;
            }
        }
    }

    my $result = 0;

    # Count every occurrence of the digit 5 in the retained numbers.
    for my $num (@seq) {
        my $text = "$num";
        my $count = () = ($text =~ /5/g);
        $result += $count;
    }

    return $result;
}
```

This preserves the Python code’s behavior. Note that `18` is **not** a multiple of both 9 and 14; their least common multiple is `126`. Thus, the loop checks every 18th number and relies on the `if` statement to filter candidates. Also, as written, the starting value may equal `n`, despite the comment saying numbers should be less than `n`.

