Because revisiting is allowed, every move always has a valid continuation. Thus the lexicographically smallest path starts at the cell containing `1` and then greedily moves to the adjacent cell with the smallest value.

```rust
pub fn min_path(grid: Vec<Vec<i32>>, k: i32) -> Vec<i32> {
    assert!(k > 0);

    let n = grid.len();
    assert!(n >= 2);

    // Find the cell containing 1, which must start the minimum path.
    let mut current = None;
    for r in 0..n {
        for c in 0..n {
            if grid[r][c] == 1 {
                current = Some((r, c));
                break;
            }
        }
        if current.is_some() {
            break;
        }
    }

    let (mut row, mut col) = current.expect("grid must contain 1");
    let mut path = Vec::with_capacity(k as usize);
    let directions = [(-1isize, 0isize), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)];

    for _ in 0..k {
        path.push(grid[row][col]);

        let mut best_value = i32::MAX;
        let mut best_position = (row, col);

        for &(dr, dc) in &directions {
            let nr = row as isize + dr;
            let nc = col as isize + dc;

            if nr >= 0 && nr < n as isize && nc >= 0 && nc < n as isize {
                let nr = nr as usize;
                let nc = nc as usize;

                if grid[nr][nc] < best_value {
                    best_value = grid[nr][nc];
                    best_position = (nr, nc);
                }
            }
        }

        (row, col) = best_position;
    }

    path
}
```

Time complexity is `O(N² + k)`, and the returned path uses `O(k)` space.

