The coefficients are interpreted in ascending power order: `xs.get(i)` is the coefficient of \(x^i\). Since the number of coefficients is even and the leading coefficient is nonzero, the polynomial has odd degree and therefore at least one real root.

```java
import java.util.List;

public class Solution {
    public double findZero(List<Double> xs) {
        if (xs == null || xs.isEmpty() || (xs.size() & 1) != 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "The coefficient list must have a positive even size"
            );
        }

        double[] coefficients = new double[xs.size()];
        for (int i = 0; i < xs.size(); i++) {
            Double coefficient = xs.get(i);
            if (coefficient == null || !Double.isFinite(coefficient)) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Coefficients must be finite numbers"
                );
            }
            coefficients[i] = coefficient;
        }

        if (coefficients[coefficients.length - 1] == 0.0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "The highest-degree coefficient must be nonzero"
            );
        }

        // Find an interval whose endpoint values have opposite signs.
        double bound = 1.0;
        double left = -bound;
        double right = bound;
        double fLeft = evaluate(coefficients, left);
        double fRight = evaluate(coefficients, right);

        while (sameSign(fLeft, fRight)) {
            if (bound == Double.MAX_VALUE) {
                throw new ArithmeticException(
                    "No representable finite bracket for a root"
                );
            }

            bound = bound > Double.MAX_VALUE / 2.0
                    ? Double.MAX_VALUE
                    : bound * 2.0;

            left = -bound;
            right = bound;
            fLeft = evaluate(coefficients, left);
            fRight = evaluate(coefficients, right);
        }

        if (fLeft == 0.0) {
            return left;
        }
        if (fRight == 0.0) {
            return right;
        }

        // Bisection until no more representable progress can be made.
        for (int i = 0; i < 2048; i++) {
            double middle = left * 0.5 + right * 0.5;

            if (middle == left || middle == right) {
                return Math.abs(fLeft) <= Math.abs(fRight) ? left : right;
            }

            double fMiddle = evaluate(coefficients, middle);
            if (fMiddle == 0.0) {
                return middle;
            }

            if (sameSign(fLeft, fMiddle)) {
                left = middle;
                fLeft = fMiddle;
            } else {
                right = middle;
                fRight = fMiddle;
            }
        }

        return left * 0.5 + right * 0.5;
    }

    // Horner's method.
    private double evaluate(double[] coefficients, double x) {
        double result = coefficients[coefficients.length - 1];
        for (int i = coefficients.length - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            result = result * x + coefficients[i];
        }
        return result;
    }

    private boolean sameSign(double a, double b) {
        return a != 0.0 && b != 0.0 && (a < 0.0) == (b < 0.0);
    }
}
```

Final answer: Use exponential bracketing followed by bisection, as implemented above.

