Because cells may be revisited, every neighbor always permits the path to continue. Therefore, lexicographic minimization is greedy: start at the globally smallest value, then repeatedly move to the smallest-valued neighbor.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Solution {
    public List<Integer> minPath(List<List<Integer>> grid, int k) {
        int n = grid.size();

        // Find the globally smallest cell (value 1 under the given constraints).
        int row = 0;
        int col = 0;
        int minimum = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
                int value = grid.get(r).get(c);
                if (value < minimum) {
                    minimum = value;
                    row = r;
                    col = c;
                }
            }
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>(k);
        result.add(grid.get(row).get(col));

        int[][] directions = {
            {-1, 0},
            {1, 0},
            {0, -1},
            {0, 1}
        };

        for (int step = 1; step < k; step++) {
            int nextRow = -1;
            int nextCol = -1;
            int nextValue = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

            for (int[] direction : directions) {
                int nr = row + direction[0];
                int nc = col + direction[1];

                if (nr >= 0 && nr < n && nc >= 0 && nc < n) {
                    int value = grid.get(nr).get(nc);
                    if (value < nextValue) {
                        nextValue = value;
                        nextRow = nr;
                        nextCol = nc;
                    }
                }
            }

            row = nextRow;
            col = nextCol;
            result.add(nextValue);
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity is `O(N² + k)` and auxiliary space, excluding the returned list, is `O(1)`.

