```java
public boolean rightAngleTriangle(int a, int b, int c) {
    if (a <= 0 || b <= 0 || c <= 0) {
        return false;
    }

    long x = a;
    long y = b;
    long z = c;

    long largest = Math.max(x, Math.max(y, z));
    long side1;
    long side2;

    if (largest == x) {
        side1 = y;
        side2 = z;
    } else if (largest == y) {
        side1 = x;
        side2 = z;
    } else {
        side1 = x;
        side2 = y;
    }

    return side1 * side1 + side2 * side2 == largest * largest;
}
```

Using `long` for the calculations prevents overflow when squaring `int` side lengths.

Final answer: The function returns `true` exactly when the positive side lengths satisfy the Pythagorean theorem.

