The erroneous code was not included, so its exact bug cannot be identified. A common mistake is simulating only one pass through the string; surviving senators must continue acting in later rounds while preserving cyclic order.

Use two queues containing the current turn indices of Radiant and Dire senators. Whichever senator has the smaller index acts first, bans the opposing senator, and is scheduled for the next round by adding `n` to their index.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string predictPartyVictory(string senate) {
        const int n = senate.size();
        queue<int> radiant;
        queue<int> dire;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if (senate[i] == 'R')
                radiant.push(i);
            else
                dire.push(i);
        }

        while (!radiant.empty() && !dire.empty()) {
            int r = radiant.front();
            radiant.pop();

            int d = dire.front();
            dire.pop();

            if (r < d) {
                // Radiant acts first, bans this Dire senator,
                // and returns in the next round.
                radiant.push(r + n);
            } else {
                // Dire acts first, bans this Radiant senator,
                // and returns in the next round.
                dire.push(d + n);
            }
        }

        return radiant.empty() ? "Dire" : "Radiant";
    }
};
```

Each senator is inserted and removed only as needed until one party is eliminated.

- Time complexity: `O(n)`
- Space complexity: `O(n)`

Final answer: Simulate the cyclic voting order with two index queues; the party whose queue remains nonempty wins.

