result = find_unsorted(arr, element)
```

This returns the original index and uses no built-in search or sorting functions, but its worst-case time complexity is O(n).

If the list had already been sorted, recursive binary search would be O(log n):

```python
def binary_search(arr, target, left, right):
    if left > right:
        return -1

    middle = left + (right - left) // 2

    if arr[middle] == target:
        return middle
    if target < arr[middle]:
        return binary_search(arr, target, left, middle - 1)
    return binary_search(arr, target, middle + 1, right)
```

Sorting the unsorted input first would itself require at least O(n log n) with a comparison-based sort, so the complete one-time operation would not be O(log n). For the supplied list, `11` is absent.

