I don’t have personal usage experience, but Obout was historically a capable ASP.NET Web Forms control suite. Its Grid offered many features out of the box—AJAX updates, sorting, filtering, grouping, paging, templates, and editing—and could reduce development time for conventional data-entry applications.

The common trade-offs were:

- generated markup and JavaScript could be heavy and difficult to debug;
- advanced customization was less straightforward than using a simpler grid;
- documentation, examples, accessibility, and browser compatibility needed careful evaluation;
- applications became tightly coupled to a third-party Web Forms control;
- vendor support and update frequency were especially important.

For a legacy Web Forms application already using Obout, the Grid may still be serviceable if it meets your requirements. Before adopting it now, verify that the exact product is actively maintained, supports your target .NET and browser versions, and has dependable licensing and support. For a new project, I would not select it without that verification; I would generally prefer a currently maintained grid or a modern ASP.NET Core/front-end solution. A proof of concept using your largest dataset, editing workflow, and required browsers is more useful than a numerical rating.

Final answer: Historically feature-rich and adequate for standard ASP.NET Web Forms grids, but its maintenance status, support, performance, and vendor lock-in make it a risky choice for a new application.

