PSR-7 defines the request/response messages but not an asynchronous callback API. The following example uses ReactPHP promises for asynchronous handling and PSR-17 factories to create PSR-7 responses.

```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseFactoryInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\StreamFactoryInterface;
use React\Promise\PromiseInterface;

use function React\Promise\resolve;

/**
 * @param callable(string, float): (PromiseInterface|mixed) $saveFruit
 *
 * @return callable(ServerRequestInterface): PromiseInterface<ResponseInterface>
 */
function createFruitPostHandler(
    ResponseFactoryInterface $responseFactory,
    StreamFactoryInterface $streamFactory,
    callable $saveFruit
): callable {
    /**
     * @param array<string, mixed> $payload
     */
    $jsonResponse = static function (
        int $status,
        array $payload
    ) use ($responseFactory, $streamFactory): ResponseInterface {
        $json = json_encode($payload, JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR);

        return $responseFactory
            ->createResponse($status)
            ->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json; charset=utf-8')
            ->withBody($streamFactory->createStream($json));
    };

    return static function (
        ServerRequestInterface $request
    ) use ($saveFruit, $jsonResponse): PromiseInterface {
        // Starting from a resolved promise ensures that the callback always
        // returns asynchronously through the PromiseInterface.
        return resolve(null)->then(
            static function () use ($request, $saveFruit, $jsonResponse) {
                if (strtoupper($request->getMethod()) !== 'POST') {
                    return $jsonResponse(405, [
                        'error' => 'Only POST requests are allowed.',
                    ])->withHeader('Allow', 'POST');
                }

                $data = $request->getParsedBody();

                if (!is_array($data)) {
                    return $jsonResponse(400, [
                        'error' => 'The request body must contain valid POST data.',
                    ]);
                }

                $fruitName = $data['fruitname'] ?? null;
                $rawPrice  = $data['price'] ?? null;

                $errors = [];

                if (!is_string($fruitName) || trim($fruitName) === '') {
                    $errors['fruitname'] = 'fruitname must be a non-empty string.';
                }

                $validPriceType =
                    is_int($rawPrice) ||
                    is_float($rawPrice) ||
                    is_string($rawPrice);

                if (
                    !$validPriceType ||
                    !is_numeric($rawPrice) ||
                    !is_finite((float) $rawPrice)
                ) {
                    $errors['price'] = 'price must be a finite numeric value.';
                } elseif ((float) $rawPrice < 0) {
                    $errors['price'] = 'price must not be negative.';
                }

                if ($errors !== []) {
                    return $jsonResponse(422, [
                        'error'  => 'Validation failed.',
                        'fields' => $errors,
                    ]);
                }

                $fruitName = trim($fruitName);
                $price = (float) $rawPrice;

                // $saveFruit may perform asynchronous database or API work and
                // may return either a PromiseInterface or an immediate value.
                return resolve($saveFruit($fruitName, $price))->then(
                    static fn (mixed $result): ResponseInterface =>
                        $jsonResponse(201, [
                            'message' => 'Fruit saved successfully.',
                            'fruit'   => [
                                'fruitname' => $fruitName,
                                'price'     => $price,
                            ],
                            'result' => $result,
                        ])
                );
            }
        );
    };
}
```

Example dependencies:

```bash
composer require psr/http-message psr/http-factory react/promise nyholm/psr7
```

The server or framework must parse form or JSON input and expose it through `ServerRequestInterface::getParsedBody()`.

Final answer: The callback above asynchronously validates PSR-7 POST requests, requires non-empty `fruitname`, rejects invalid or negative `price` values, invokes the supplied asynchronous handler, and returns PSR-7 JSON responses.

