ground_truth:
`SharePointOnlineCredentials` uses username/password authentication. Azure/Entra security defaults block this legacy flow and usually require MFA, so changing `SecurityProtocol` will not solve the issue.

For unattended code, use PnP Framework with an Entra ID application and certificate-based app-only authentication.

### 1. Install PnP Framework

```powershell
Install-Package PnP.Framework
```

### 2. Configure an Entra ID application

1. Register an application in Entra ID.
2. Upload the public part of an X.509 certificate.
3. Add an appropriate **SharePoint application permission**, preferably:
   - `Sites.Selected` for access only to explicitly authorized sites, or
   - `Sites.FullControl.All` if broad tenant-wide access is genuinely required.
4. Grant tenant administrator consent.
5. If using `Sites.Selected`, separately grant the application access to the target site.

### 3. Authenticate using the certificate

```csharp
using Microsoft.SharePoint.Client;
using PnP.Framework;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;

string siteUrl = "https://abc.sharepoint.com/sites/app";
string tenantId = "your-tenant-id";       // GUID or tenant domain
string clientId = "your-application-id";
string certificatePath = @"C:\Certificates\sharepoint-app.pfx";
string certificatePassword = "certificate-password";

var certificate = new X509Certificate2(
    certificatePath,
    certificatePassword,
    X509KeyStorageFlags.MachineKeySet |
    X509KeyStorageFlags.Exportable);

using (AuthenticationManager authenticationManager =
       AuthenticationManager.CreateWithCertificate(
           clientId,
           certificate,
           tenantId))
{
    using (ClientContext context =
           authenticationManager.GetContext(siteUrl))
    {
        context.Load(context.Web, web => web.Url);
        context.ExecuteQuery();

        Console.WriteLine(context.Web.Url);
    }
}
```

In production, load the certificate from the Windows certificate store or Azure Key Vault rather than keeping a PFX password in source code.

If the application runs in an Azure service that supports managed identities, PnP Framework can also use managed identity:

```csharp
using PnP.Framework;
using Microsoft.SharePoint.Client;

string siteUrl = "https://abc.sharepoint.com/sites/app";

using (AuthenticationManager authenticationManager =
       AuthenticationManager.CreateWithManagedIdentity())
{
    using (ClientContext context =
           authenticationManager.GetContext(siteUrl))
    {
        context.Load(context.Web, web => web.Url);
        context.ExecuteQuery();

        Console.WriteLine(context.Web.Url);
    }
}
```

The managed identity must also be assigned the necessary SharePoint application permissions/site grant.

For an interactive desktop utility, an interactive or device-login flow can be used instead, allowing the user to complete MFA. It is not appropriate for unattended services. Do not attempt to bypass security defaults by retaining the administrator username and password.

Final answer: Replace `SharePointOnlineCredentials` with PnP Framework modern authentication, preferably certificate-based app-only authentication or managed identity, and grant the corresponding Entra application the required SharePoint permissions.

