Java’s `Base64` decoder and a `Map<String, Rgb>` provide the equivalent behavior. Since the identifiers in the Python example are placeholders, the special-color map can be passed into the method:

```java
import java.util.Base64;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public final class ColorDecoder {

    public record Rgb(int red, int green, int blue) {
        public Rgb {
            if (red < 0 || red > 255
                    || green < 0 || green > 255
                    || blue < 0 || blue > 255) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                        "RGB components must be between 0 and 255");
            }
        }
    }

    public static Map<String, Rgb> decodeColors(
            String colorString,
            Map<String, Rgb> identifierToRgb) {

        byte[] bytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(colorString);

        // Each color must contain exactly three bytes.
        if (bytes.length % 3 != 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Decoded data length must be a multiple of 3");
        }

        Map<String, Rgb> colorMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < bytes.length; i += 3) {
            int red = bytes[i] & 0xFF;
            int green = bytes[i + 1] & 0xFF;
            int blue = bytes[i + 2] & 0xFF;

            // Lowercase, six-character hexadecimal code, like Python bytes.hex().
            String colorCode = String.format(
                    "%02x%02x%02x", red, green, blue);

            Rgb decodedRgb = new Rgb(red, green, blue);
            Rgb mappedRgb = identifierToRgb.getOrDefault(
                    colorCode, decodedRgb);

            colorMap.put(colorCode, mappedRgb);
        }

        return colorMap;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Replace these example identifiers with the actual six-character
        // hexadecimal identifiers used by your input format.
        Map<String, Rgb> specialColors = Map.of(
                "a1b2c3", new Rgb(255, 255, 255), // white
                "d4e5f6", new Rgb(0, 0, 0),       // black
                "123456", new Rgb(255, 0, 0)      // red
        );

        String encodedColors = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(
                new byte[]{
                        (byte) 0xA1, (byte) 0xB2, (byte) 0xC3,
                        (byte) 0x12, (byte) 0x34, (byte) 0x56,
                        (byte) 0x10, (byte) 0x20, (byte) 0x30
                });

        Map<String, Rgb> colors =
                decodeColors(encodedColors, specialColors);

        colors.forEach((code, rgb) ->
                System.out.println(code + " -> " + rgb));
    }
}
```

`LinkedHashMap` retains the order in which colors occur. As in the Python dictionary, repeated color codes overwrite earlier entries.

Also, the three `'___'` keys in the Python example are identical, so only the last one survives. They must be replaced with distinct identifiers for all three mappings to work.

Final answer: Use `Base64.getDecoder().decode`, process the decoded bytes in groups of three, format each group as a six-character hexadecimal key, and use `Map.getOrDefault` to apply special RGB mappings.

