There is no leak in this case. `PreviewKeyDown` handlers are stored by the `UIElement` itself. If the handler is a static method, the reference chain is effectively:

```text
UIElement -> event-handler delegate -> static method
```

Nothing in that chain roots the `UIElement`, so when the element is no longer referenced by the visual tree or elsewhere, it and its event-handler storage can be collected.

Continue to detach the handler when the attached property is changed back to `false`:

```csharp
private static void OnIsEnabledChanged(
    DependencyObject d, DependencyPropertyChangedEventArgs e)
{
    if (d is not UIElement element)
        return;

    if ((bool)e.OldValue)
        element.PreviewKeyDown -= OnPreviewKeyDown;

    if ((bool)e.NewValue)
        element.PreviewKeyDown += OnPreviewKeyDown;
}
```

Removing before adding also prevents accidental duplicate registration.

Do not keep a global list of managed elements: such a list would itself strongly reference the controls and create the very leak you are trying to avoid. Unhooking during `Application.Exit` is also unnecessary because the process and its entire object graph are ending.

Event subscriptions become a leak concern when a longer-lived publisher—such as `Application`, a singleton service, static event, timer, or global event aggregator—holds a handler whose target is a shorter-lived object. In those cases, explicitly unsubscribe or use an appropriate weak-event mechanism. An element subscribing a static handler to its own event does not have that problem.

Final answer: No cleanup list or `Application.Exit` handling is needed; detach when `IsEnabled` becomes false, and otherwise let the `UIElement` and its handler be garbage-collected together.

A subsequence need not be contiguous, so “begin position” is interpreted as the zero-based position of the first selected character in each string. When several LCS solutions exist, this implementation deterministically prefers matching the current characters, then skipping characters from earlier input strings.

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
#include <string>
#include <type_traits>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

struct LCSResult {
    std::string subsequence;

    // positions[i] is the index of the first selected character in strings[i].
    // It is std::string::npos if the LCS is empty.
    std::vector<std::size_t> positions;
};

struct VectorHash {
    std::size_t operator()(const std::vector<std::size_t>& values) const noexcept {
        std::size_t hash = 0;

        for (std::size_t value : values) {
            hash ^= std::hash<std::size_t>{}(value)
                  + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL
                  + (hash << 6)
                  + (hash >> 2);
        }

        return hash;
    }
};

LCSResult longestCommonSubsequenceMany(
    const std::vector<std::string>& strings) {

    const std::size_t stringCount = strings.size();

    if (stringCount == 0) {
        return {"", {}};
    }

    using State = std::vector<std::size_t>;

    std::unordered_map<State, std::size_t, VectorHash> memo;

    /*
     * solve(position) returns the LCS length for the suffixes beginning
     * at position[i] in strings[i].
     */
    std::function<std::size_t(const State&)> solve =
        [&](const State& position) -> std::size_t {
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < stringCount; ++i) {
                if (position[i] == strings[i].size()) {
                    return 0;
                }
            }

            auto found = memo.find(position);
            if (found != memo.end()) {
                return found->second;
            }

            std::size_t best = 0;

            // Option 1: match the current character in every string.
            bool allEqual = true;
            const char current = strings[0][position[0]];

            for (std::size_t i = 1; i < stringCount; ++i) {
                if (strings[i][position[i]] != current) {
                    allEqual = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (allEqual) {
                State next = position;
                for (std::size_t& index : next) {
                    ++index;
                }
                best = 1 + solve(next);
            }

            /*
             * Option 2: skip the current character in one of the strings.
             * These branches also cover optimal solutions that do not use
             * an equal current character.
             */
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < stringCount; ++i) {
                State next = position;
                ++next[i];
                best = std::max(best, solve(next));
            }

            memo.emplace(position, best);
            return best;
        };

    State position(stringCount, 0);
    const std::size_t lcsLength = solve(position);

    LCSResult result;
    result.subsequence.reserve(lcsLength);
    result.positions.assign(stringCount, std::string::npos);

    // Reconstruct one optimal LCS from the memoized recursion.
    while (solve(position) != 0) {
        const std::size_t best = solve(position);

        bool allEqual = true;
        const char current = strings[0][position[0]];

        for (std::size_t i = 1; i < stringCount; ++i) {
            if (strings[i][position[i]] != current) {
                allEqual = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (allEqual) {
            State next = position;
            for (std::size_t& index : next) {
                ++index;
            }

            if (1 + solve(next) == best) {
                if (result.subsequence.empty()) {
                    result.positions = position;
                }

                result.subsequence.push_back(current);
                position = std::move(next);
                continue;
            }
        }

        // Find a skip that preserves the optimal length.
        bool advanced = false;

        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < stringCount; ++i) {
            State next = position;
            ++next[i];

            if (solve(next) == best) {
                position = std::move(next);
                advanced = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Defensive check; a valid DP state always has an optimal transition.
        if (!advanced) {
            break;
        }
    }

    return result;
}

/*
 * Variadic wrapper. It accepts three strings as requested, but also supports
 * any other number of string-compatible arguments.
 */
template <typename... StringTypes>
LCSResult longestCommonSubsequence(const StringTypes&... inputs) {
    static_assert(
        (std::is_constructible_v<std::string, const StringTypes&> && ...),
        "Every argument must be convertible to std::string");

    return longestCommonSubsequenceMany(
        std::vector<std::string>{std::string(inputs)...});
}

int main() {
    std::string s1 = "Hello there!";
    std::string s2 = "Hi there!";
    std::string s3 = "Hey there!";

    LCSResult result = longestCommonSubsequence(s1, s2, s3);

    std::cout << "LCS: \"" << result.subsequence << "\"\n";
    std::cout << "Beginning positions: ";

    for (std::size_t position : result.positions) {
        if (position == std::string::npos) {
            std::cout << "npos ";
        } else {
            std::cout << position << ' ';
        }
    }

    std::cout << '\n';
}
```

The memoized recursive state consists of one position per input string. For \(k\) strings of lengths \(n_1,\ldots,n_k\), the time complexity is \(O(k\prod_i(n_i+1))\), with \(O(\prod_i(n_i+1))\) memoization space. If any input is empty, the returned subsequence is empty and every corresponding beginning position is `std::string::npos`.

Final answer: For the sample inputs, the LCS is `"H there!"`, beginning at zero-based position `0` in all three strings.

