The specification and examples are inconsistent. In particular, no standard meaning of “odd position in the string” produces all three outputs.

The last two examples are consistent with shifting letters at **odd positions in the alphabet** (`A/a = 1`, `C/c = 3`, etc.). Under that interpretation, the first result should be `"bbd"`, not `"bbc"`.

```js
const moveChars = (s, n) => {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n)) {
    throw new TypeError("n must be an integer");
  }

  // Normalize negative and large shifts to 0–25.
  const shift = ((n % 26) + 26) % 26;
  let result = "";

  for (const char of s) {
    const code = char.charCodeAt(0);

    const base =
      code >= 65 && code <= 90 ? 65 :   // A-Z
      code >= 97 && code <= 122 ? 97 :  // a-z
      null;

    if (base === null) {
      result += char; // Preserve non-alphabetic characters.
      continue;
    }

    const alphabetIndex = code - base; // A/a = 0, B/b = 1, ...

    // An even zero-based index is an odd one-based alphabet position.
    if (alphabetIndex % 2 === 0) {
      result += String.fromCharCode(
        base + ((alphabetIndex + shift) % 26)
      );
    } else {
      result += char;
    }
  }

  return result;
};

console.log(moveChars("abc", 1));    // "bbd"
console.log(moveChars("a1c", 2));    // "c1e"
console.log(moveChars("AB2C", -1));  // "ZB2B"
```

This runs in \(O(|s|)\) time and preserves letter case and all non-alphabetic characters. The stated `"bbc"` result would need to be corrected to `"bbd"` for the rules to be consistent.

