What's the best way to use multi Semantics if I don't have access to the first semantics in the widget tree?
Code for example:

Semantics(
      focused: true,
      child: Container(
        color: Colors.red,
        child: Align(
          child: Semantics(
            header: true,
            child: Text('test semantics'),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    );

ground_truth:
`Semantics` annotations can be nested. With the default `container: false`, Flutter generally combines their properties into the same semantics node, so your example can expose one node that is both focused and a header.

```dart
Semantics(
  focused: isFocused,
  child: Container(
    color: Colors.red,
    child: Align(
      child: Semantics(
        header: true,
        child: const Text('test semantics'),
      ),
    ),
  ),
);
```

If an intermediate widget creates its own semantics boundary (`container: true`) or the subtree creates multiple semantic nodes, wrap the whole subtree in `MergeSemantics`:

```dart
MergeSemantics(
  child: Semantics(
    focused: isFocused,
    child: Container(
      color: Colors.red,
      child: Align(
        child: Semantics(
          header: true,
          child: const Text('test semantics'),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  ),
);
```

Use `MergeSemantics` carefully: it intentionally collapses all descendant semantics into one accessibility node, which is inappropriate if the subtree contains multiple independently actionable controls.

Also, `focused: true` only reports focus state to accessibility services; it does not request or move focus. Normally it should reflect a `FocusNode`’s state rather than be hard-coded.

Final answer: Nest non-container `Semantics` widgets; their properties will normally combine. Use `MergeSemantics` only when semantic boundaries or multiple descendant nodes must explicitly be collapsed into one.

A min-heap can track the smallest unmerged node from each list. Nodes are relinked directly, so no new list nodes are allocated.

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <queue>
#include <vector>

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;

    explicit Node(int v) : value(v), next(this), prev(this) {}
};

/*
 * Merges k disjoint, sorted circular doubly linked lists.
 *
 * Contract:
 * - Each non-null head points to the smallest node in its list.
 * - Following next from a head visits nodes in nondecreasing order
 *   until returning to that head.
 * - The input lists are disjoint.
 *
 * The input lists are consumed: their nodes are relinked into the result.
 * Returns nullptr if all input lists are empty.
 */
Node* mergeSortedCircularLists(const std::vector<Node*>& lists) {
    struct Entry {
        Node* node;      // Current node from this input list
        Node* stop;      // Original head; marks the end of one traversal
        std::size_t id;  // Deterministic tie-breaker
    };

    struct Greater {
        bool operator()(const Entry& a, const Entry& b) const {
            if (a.node->value != b.node->value) {
                return a.node->value > b.node->value;
            }
            return a.id > b.id;
        }
    };

    std::priority_queue<Entry, std::vector<Entry>, Greater> minHeap;

    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < lists.size(); ++i) {
        if (lists[i] != nullptr) {
            minHeap.push({lists[i], lists[i], i});
        }
    }

    Node* resultHead = nullptr;
    Node* resultTail = nullptr;

    while (!minHeap.empty()) {
        Entry current = minHeap.top();
        minHeap.pop();

        Node* node = current.node;

        // Save the original successor before relinking this node.
        Node* successor = node->next;

        // Queue the next node from this same input list, unless the
        // traversal has wrapped back to its original head.
        if (successor != current.stop) {
            minHeap.push({successor, current.stop, current.id});
        }

        // Append node to the circular result list.
        if (resultHead == nullptr) {
            resultHead = resultTail = node;
            node->next = node;
            node->prev = node;
        } else {
            node->prev = resultTail;
            node->next = resultHead;
            resultTail->next = node;
            resultHead->prev = node;
            resultTail = node;
        }
    }

    return resultHead;
}
```

For \(N\) total nodes across \(k\) lists, the time complexity is \(O(N\log k)\), and the auxiliary space complexity is \(O(k)\).

Final answer: Use the min-heap-based `mergeSortedCircularLists` function above to merge the lists while preserving circular doubly linked structure and sorted order.

