Drive letters are DOS-device symbolic links. Use `QueryDosDevice()` to inspect those links and find which drive letter maps to the NT device path.

For example, conceptually:

```text
QueryDosDevice(L"G:", buffer, size)
    -> L"\Device\CdRom0"
```

To find the letter, enumerate the logical drives and test each one:

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <cwchar>
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::optional<std::wstring>
DevicePathToDrivePath(const std::wstring& devicePath)
{
    wchar_t drives[512];

    DWORD n = GetLogicalDriveStringsW(
        static_cast<DWORD>(std::size(drives)), drives);

    if (n == 0 || n >= std::size(drives))
        return std::nullopt;

    size_t bestPrefix = 0;
    std::optional<std::wstring> result;

    // drives is a MULTI_SZ such as "C:\\\0D:\\\0G:\\\0\0".
    for (const wchar_t* root = drives; *root; root += wcslen(root) + 1)
    {
        wchar_t driveName[3] = { root[0], L':', L'\0' };
        std::vector<wchar_t> targets(32768);

        DWORD targetLength = QueryDosDeviceW(
            driveName, targets.data(),
            static_cast<DWORD>(targets.size()));

        if (targetLength == 0)
            continue;

        // QueryDosDevice can return more than one target as a MULTI_SZ.
        for (const wchar_t* target = targets.data();
             *target;
             target += wcslen(target) + 1)
        {
            size_t len = wcslen(target);

            // Match the device itself or a path below it, choosing the
            // longest match in case mappings overlap.
            bool matches =
                devicePath.size() >= len &&
                _wcsnicmp(devicePath.c_str(), target, len) == 0 &&
                (devicePath.size() == len || devicePath[len] == L'\\');

            if (!matches || len < bestPrefix)
                continue;

            std::wstring dosPath(root); // e.g. "G:\\"

            if (devicePath.size() > len) {
                size_t suffix = len;
                if (devicePath[suffix] == L'\\')
                    ++suffix;
                dosPath.append(devicePath, suffix, std::wstring::npos);
            }

            bestPrefix = len;
            result = std::move(dosPath);
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```

For `\Device\CdRom0`, this returns `G:\` if `G:` currently maps to that device.

A device is not guaranteed to have a drive letter, and it may have more than one DOS path. Volume mount-point enumeration is needed if mounted-directory paths must also be found, but `QueryDosDevice()` is the direct API for resolving an NT device path against drive-letter mappings.

Final answer: Enumerate drive letters and call `QueryDosDevice(L"X:", ...)`; the letter whose returned target equals `\Device\CdRom0` is the corresponding logical drive.

