Flex/Flash Player has no native PDF renderer, so a browser-hosted Flex application cannot directly display a PDF inside a normal Flex component.

For an **Adobe AIR desktop application**, the simplest option is AIR’s `HTML`/`HTMLLoader` control:

```xml
<mx:HTML id="pdfView" width="100%" height="100%" />
```

```actionscript
import flash.html.HTMLLoader;
import flash.html.HTMLPDFCapability;

if (HTMLLoader.pdfCapability == HTMLPDFCapability.STATUS_OK) {
    pdfView.location = "https://example.com/document.pdf";
} else {
    // Prompt the user to install/update Adobe Reader,
    // or open/download the PDF externally.
}
```

This does not use IE or Firefox, but it does require a compatible Adobe Reader installation; AIR itself does not render the PDF.

If this is a browser-based Flex application, the practical choices are:

- Open the PDF in the system/browser PDF viewer with `navigateToURL()`.
- Place an HTML `<iframe>` beside/over the Flex application, which still depends on the browser’s PDF support.
- Convert the PDF server-side to images or SWF and build page navigation in Flex.
- Use a commercial PDF-rendering/conversion component.

If dependency on both browsers and Adobe Reader is unacceptable, server-side conversion to page images is generally the most predictable and maintainable integrated solution.

Final answer: Flex cannot natively render PDFs; use AIR `HTMLLoader` with Adobe Reader, open the PDF externally, or convert it to images/SWF for a fully integrated viewer.

Go does not have a built-in `set` type. The idiomatic representation is a map whose keys are the set elements. `struct{}` is commonly used as the value because it occupies no storage.

```go
package main

import "fmt"

// Set represents a set of values.
//
// Go map keys must be "comparable", so T is constrained accordingly.
// Strings, integers, pointers, and structs containing comparable fields
// are examples of comparable types.
type Set[T comparable] map[T]struct{}

// subsetCheck reports whether every element in s1 also exists in s2.
func subsetCheck[T comparable](s1, s2 Set[T]) bool {
	// Python:
	//     for ele in s1:
	//
	// Ranging over a map iterates over its keys. Because the keys are
	// the set elements, ele is one element from s1.
	for ele := range s1 {
		// Looking up a map key can return two values:
		//   _     = the stored value, which we do not need
		//   found = whether the key exists in the map
		_, found := s2[ele]

		// Python:
		//     if ele not in s2:
		if !found {
			// If one element of s1 is missing from s2,
			// s1 cannot be a subset of s2.
			return false
		}
	}

	// If every element was found, s1 is a subset of s2.
	return true
}

func main() {
	s1 := Set[int]{
		1: {},
		2: {},
	}

	s2 := Set[int]{
		1: {},
		2: {},
		3: {},
	}

	fmt.Println(subsetCheck(s1, s2)) // true
	fmt.Println(subsetCheck(s2, s1)) // false
}
```

The closest line-by-line translation of the Python function is therefore:

```go
func subsetCheck[T comparable](s1, s2 map[T]struct{}) bool {
	for ele := range s1 {
		if _, found := s2[ele]; !found {
			return false
		}
	}
	return true
}
```

As in Python, an empty `s1` is a subset of any `s2`, so the function returns `true` when there are no elements to check.

Final answer: Represent a Go set as `map[T]struct{}`, iterate with `for ele := range s1`, and test membership with `_, found := s2[ele]`.

A DAO does not require multiple inheritance—or even inheritance at all. Keep domain objects independent of persistence, give each aggregate a small DAO interface, and inject an implementation using composition.

```cpp
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

using UserId = long long;

struct User {
    UserId id;
    std::string name;
};

// Persistence contract
class UserDao {
public:
    virtual ~UserDao() = default;

    virtual std::optional<User> find(UserId id) = 0;
    virtual std::vector<User> findAll() = 0;
    virtual void insert(const User& user) = 0;
    virtual void update(const User& user) = 0;
    virtual void remove(UserId id) = 0;
};

// Application code depends only on the DAO contract.
class UserService {
public:
    explicit UserService(UserDao& dao) : dao_(dao) {}

    void rename(UserId id, std::string newName)
    {
        auto user = dao_.find(id);
        if (!user)
            return; // Or throw a domain-specific exception.

        user->name = std::move(newName);
        dao_.update(*user);
    }

private:
    UserDao& dao_; // Non-owning dependency
};
```

