A directed cycle is found during DFS when an edge points to a vertex that is currently on the recursion stack (a “gray” vertex). The vertices between that ancestor and the current vertex form the cycle.

To remove cycles without deleting vertices, the implementation below removes the back edge that closed each detected cycle. It prints the cycle first, removes one edge, and repeats until a complete DFS finds no cycles.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DirectedCycleRemoval {

    private enum Color {
        WHITE, // Not visited
        GRAY,  // Currently on the DFS stack
        BLACK  // Fully processed
    }

    public static final class DirectedGraph {
        /*
         * LinkedHashMap/LinkedHashSet are used only to make traversal and
         * printed output deterministic.
         */
        private final Map<Integer, LinkedHashSet<Integer>> adjacency =
                new LinkedHashMap<>();

        // Must be called for isolated vertices.
        public void addNode(int node) {
            adjacency.computeIfAbsent(node, ignored -> new LinkedHashSet<>());
        }

        public void addEdge(int from, int to) {
            addNode(from);
            addNode(to);
            adjacency.get(from).add(to);
        }

        public boolean removeEdge(int from, int to) {
            Set<Integer> edges = adjacency.get(from);
            return edges != null && edges.remove(to);
        }

        public Set<Integer> vertices() {
            return Collections.unmodifiableSet(adjacency.keySet());
        }

        public Set<Integer> neighbors(int node) {
            Set<Integer> result = adjacency.get(node);
            return result == null
                    ? Collections.emptySet()
                    : Collections.unmodifiableSet(result);
        }

        public void printGraph() {
            for (int node : adjacency.keySet()) {
                System.out.println(node + " -> " + adjacency.get(node));
            }
        }
    }

    /*
     * A detected cycle and the back edge that closed it.
     * The cycle list repeats its first vertex at the end.
     * For example: [1, 2, 3, 1].
     */
    private static final class Cycle {
        final List<Integer> vertices;
        final int edgeFrom;
        final int edgeTo;

        Cycle(List<Integer> vertices, int edgeFrom, int edgeTo) {
            this.vertices = vertices;
            this.edgeFrom = edgeFrom;
            this.edgeTo = edgeTo;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Finds, displays, and breaks cycles until the graph is acyclic.
     *
     * @return the concrete cycles found during the removal process
     */
    public static List<List<Integer>> detectAndRemoveCycles(
            DirectedGraph graph) {

        List<List<Integer>> removedCycles = new ArrayList<>();

        while (true) {
            Cycle cycle = findOneCycle(graph);

            if (cycle == null) {
                break;
            }

            // Show the cycle before changing the graph.
            System.out.println("Detected cycle: "
                    + formatCycle(cycle.vertices));

            // Removing the back edge is enough to break this cycle.
            System.out.println("Removing edge: "
                    + cycle.edgeFrom + " -> " + cycle.edgeTo);

            graph.removeEdge(cycle.edgeFrom, cycle.edgeTo);
            removedCycles.add(new ArrayList<>(cycle.vertices));
        }

        return removedCycles;
    }

    /**
     * Searches all connected components for one directed cycle.
     */
    private static Cycle findOneCycle(DirectedGraph graph) {
        Map<Integer, Color> colors = new HashMap<>();

        /*
         * stack holds the current DFS path. stackPosition allows the cycle
         * path to be extracted when an edge reaches a gray vertex.
         */
        List<Integer> stack = new ArrayList<>();
        Map<Integer, Integer> stackPosition = new HashMap<>();

        for (int node : graph.vertices()) {
            if (colors.getOrDefault(node, Color.WHITE) == Color.WHITE) {
                Cycle cycle = dfs(
                        node, graph, colors, stack, stackPosition);

                if (cycle != null) {
                    return cycle;
                }
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    private static Cycle dfs(
            int current,
            DirectedGraph graph,
            Map<Integer, Color> colors,
            List<Integer> stack,
            Map<Integer, Integer> stackPosition) {

        colors.put(current, Color.GRAY);
        stackPosition.put(current, stack.size());
        stack.add(current);

        for (int next : graph.neighbors(current)) {
            Color nextColor = colors.getOrDefault(next, Color.WHITE);

            if (nextColor == Color.WHITE) {
                Cycle cycle = dfs(
                        next, graph, colors, stack, stackPosition);

                if (cycle != null) {
                    return cycle;
                }
            } else if (nextColor == Color.GRAY) {
                /*
                 * next is an ancestor on the active path:
                 *
                 * next -> ... -> current -> next
                 */
                int start = stackPosition.get(next);
                List<Integer> cycleVertices = new ArrayList<>();

                for (int i = start; i < stack.size(); i++) {
                    cycleVertices.add(stack.get(i));
                }

                // Repeat the first node to show the closing edge.
                cycleVertices.add(next);

                return new Cycle(cycleVertices, current, next);
            }
        }

        stack.remove(stack.size() - 1);
        stackPosition.remove(current);
        colors.put(current, Color.BLACK);
        return null;
    }

    private static String formatCycle(List<Integer> cycle) {
        StringJoiner joiner = new StringJoiner(" -> ");
        for (int node : cycle) {
            joiner.add(Integer.toString(node));
        }
        return joiner.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DirectedGraph graph = new DirectedGraph();

        // First component, containing two cycles.
        graph.addEdge(1, 2);
        graph.addEdge(2, 3);
        graph.addEdge(3, 1); // 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1
        graph.addEdge(3, 2); // 2 -> 3 -> 2

        // A disconnected component containing another cycle.
        graph.addEdge(4, 5);
        graph.addEdge(5, 6);
        graph.addEdge(6, 4); // 4 -> 5 -> 6 -> 4

        // An acyclic disconnected component.
        graph.addEdge(7, 8);

        // An isolated vertex.
        graph.addNode(99);

        System.out.println("Original graph:");
        graph.printGraph();

        System.out.println("\nCycle removal:");
        List<List<Integer>> cycles =
                detectAndRemoveCycles(graph);

        System.out.println("\nCycles broken: " + cycles.size());

        System.out.println("\nResulting acyclic graph:");
        graph.printGraph();
    }
}
```

### How it works

- `WHITE` means a node has not yet been visited.
- `GRAY` means it is on the current DFS recursion path.
- `BLACK` means its DFS processing is complete.
- An edge from the current node to a `GRAY` node closes a directed cycle.
- The active DFS stack is used to reconstruct and print that cycle.
- The closing edge is then removed, preserving all vertices.
- DFS is repeated until no cycle can be found, at which point the graph is a DAG.

### Isolated nodes and disconnected structures

An isolated node must be added explicitly with `addNode`, because it has no edge that would otherwise register it. The outer loop in `findOneCycle` starts DFS from every unvisited vertex, so disconnected components and multiple disconnected cycles are all examined.

If `r` edges are removed, this straightforward implementation takes `O((r + 1)(V + E))` time and `O(V)` DFS memory. At most `E` edges can be removed.

This reports every cycle encountered and broken during removal. It does not necessarily enumerate every distinct simple cycle that existed in the original graph: removing one edge can destroy several overlapping cycles at once. Enumerating all original simple cycles requires a cycle-enumeration algorithm such as Johnson’s algorithm and can require exponential output.

To create a C++ program that calculates the arithmetic mean of a series of numbers, follow these steps:

### 1. Define the calculation

For \(n\) numerical inputs \(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n\), the arithmetic mean is:

\[
\text{mean}=\frac{x_1+x_2+\cdots+x_n}{n}
\]

The program must therefore:

1. Read the number of values.
2. Validate that the count is positive.
3. Read and add the values.
4. Divide the sum by the count.
5. Display the result.

### 2. Choose appropriate data types

- Use `std::size_t` for the number of inputs.
- Use `long double` for the values, sum, and mean so that fractional and relatively large values can be handled with good precision.

### 3. Implement input validation

The program should reject:

- A missing or invalid count.
- A count of zero, because division by zero is undefined.
- Missing or non-numerical values in the series.

### 4. Complete C++ program

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    std::size_t count;

    std::cout << "Enter the number of values: ";
    if (!(std::cin >> count) || count == 0)
    {
        std::cerr << "Error: the number of values must be a positive integer.\n";
        return 1;
    }

    long double sum = 0.0L;

    std::cout << "Enter " << count << " numerical values:\n";

    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i)
    {
        long double value;

        if (!(std::cin >> value))
        {
            std::cerr << "Error: invalid value at position " << (i + 1) << ".\n";
            return 1;
        }

        sum += value;
    }

    const long double mean = sum / static_cast<long double>(count);

    std::cout << std::setprecision(12)
              << "Arithmetic mean: " << mean << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

### 5. Example execution

```text
Enter the number of values: 5
Enter 5 numerical values:
2 4 6 8 10
Arithmetic mean: 6
```

Here, the sum is \(30\), and \(30 / 5 = 6\).

### 6. Test important cases

Test the program with:

- Positive integers: `2 4 6`
- Decimal values: `1.5 2.5 3.5`
- Negative values: `-4 -2 3`
- A single value: its mean should equal that value
- Zero as the count: the program should report an error
- Non-numerical input: the program should report an error

For extremely large datasets or values with very different magnitudes, a compensated summation method such as Kahan summation can be used to reduce floating-point accumulation error.

Final answer: Read a positive count, accumulate that many numerical values, compute `mean = sum / count`, and output the result while validating all input and preventing division by zero.

A bimodal **marginal** density of the response does not, by itself, rule out a Gaussian linear mixed model. The Gaussian assumption is conditional:

\[
Y_{ij}\mid b_i,X_{ij}\sim N\!\left(X_{ij}\beta+Z_{ij}b_i,\sigma^2\right),
\]

usually together with an assumption such as \(b_i\sim N(0,D)\). It is not an assumption that all observed responses pooled across times, treatments, or subjects must have a normal density.

A pooled density can be bimodal because of:

- distinct treatment or demographic groups;
- systematic changes over time;
- other omitted covariates;
- heterogeneous clusters or latent subpopulations.

If these sources are included in the fixed- or random-effects structure, the **conditional residuals** may still be approximately Gaussian.

However, under a standard Gaussian random-effects model with Gaussian residuals, the response within a homogeneous covariate pattern has a Gaussian marginal distribution. Thus, if marked bimodality remains after conditioning on relevant predictors and cluster effects, the model is likely misspecified. Consider:

- adding missing group, time, interaction, or nonlinear effects;
- modeling heterogeneous residual variances;
- a latent-class or finite-mixture mixed model if there are genuinely distinct unobserved populations;
- another residual distribution if the issue is heavy tails or skewness—although a \(t\) or skew-normal distribution alone generally will not explain genuine bimodality.

Fit the candidate model and examine residual distributions by covariate pattern, residuals versus fitted values and time, subject-specific profiles, influential clusters, and simulation-based or posterior-predictive diagnostics. Do not choose a log transformation solely to make the pooled response histogram normal; the transformation should also yield a sensible mean–variance relationship and interpretation.

A Gaussian mixed model can still give useful estimates of conditional means under some nonnormality, especially with adequate numbers of clusters, but standard errors and prediction intervals can be sensitive; robust cluster-level standard errors or cluster bootstrap methods may help with inference, while they do not repair an incorrect mean structure.

Final answer: Yes, a Gaussian mixed model may be appropriate despite a bimodal pooled response density, but only if the distribution is approximately Gaussian after conditioning on covariates and random effects; persistent conditional bimodality calls for a richer mean structure or a mixture/latent-class mixed model.

Assuming the specified key should be placed first while preserving the order of the remaining elements:

```php
<?php

function sortByKey(array $array, string|int $key): array
{
    if (!array_key_exists($key, $array)) {
        return $array;
    }

    $value = $array[$key];
    unset($array[$key]);

    return [$key => $value] + $array;
}

$array = [
    'a' => 'Apple',
    'b' => 'Banana',
    'c' => 'Grapes',
];

$result = sortByKey($array, 'b');

print_r($result);
```

Output:

