Consider the subsequent array:
[
 {name: 'John', age: 28, city: 'New York'}, 
 {name: 'Mary', age: 34, city: 'London'}, 
 {name: 'Bob', age: 23, city: 'Los Angeles'}
]

Erroneous Code:
```javascript
function convertObjArrToCSV(arr) {
    const array = [Object.keys(arr[0])].concat(arr)

    return array.join('\n')
}
```
After identifying and fixing the error, further modify the code so that it not only processes arrays of objects of uniform structure, but also handles objects with missing or extra properties. For example, the resultant code should be able to handle the modified array below:

[
 {name: 'John', age: 28, city: 'New York', occupation: 'Engineer'}, 
 {name: 'Mary', age: 34, country: 'UK', city: 'London'}, 
 {name: 'Bob', age: 23, city: 'Los Angeles'}
]

ground_truth:
The error is that `array.join('\n')` converts each object directly to `"[object Object]"`. Each record must instead be mapped to an array of values and joined with commas.

A generalized solution can collect every property name appearing in any object, leaving an empty field when a property is missing:

```javascript
function convertObjArrToCSV(arr) {
    if (!Array.isArray(arr) || arr.length === 0) {
        return '';
    }

    // Collect all unique column names in first-seen order.
    const headers = [
        ...new Set(arr.flatMap(obj => Object.keys(obj)))
    ];

    // Quote fields and escape embedded double quotes.
    const formatField = value => {
        const text = value == null ? '' : String(value);
        return `"${text.replace(/"/g, '""')}"`;
    };

    const rows = arr.map(obj =>
        headers
            .map(header => formatField(obj[header]))
            .join(',')
    );

    return [
        headers.map(formatField).join(','),
        ...rows
    ].join('\n');
}
```

For the modified array, the output is:

```csv
