I have a usecase where in usage of elasticsearch or redis would be by the user's choice, one way of implementation is having separate class for each, which works, but isn't elegent.
Is it possible to disable a specific annotation in runtime.

ground_truth:
No. Java annotations are part of the class metadata and cannot normally be enabled or disabled at runtime. Spring Data reads these annotations while creating its mapping contexts and repositories during application-context initialization, then caches that metadata.

Instead, choose the persistence implementation at the Spring bean/configuration level.

For a deployment-wide choice, conditionally enable only one repository configuration:

```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.store", havingValue = "elasticsearch")
@EnableElasticsearchRepositories(basePackages = "com.example.elasticsearch")
class ElasticsearchConfiguration {
}

@Configuration
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.store", havingValue = "redis")
@EnableRedisRepositories(basePackages = "com.example.redis")
class RedisConfiguration {
}
```

Then set:

```properties
app.store=elasticsearch
```

Spring profiles can be used similarly.

If the choice varies per user or request, load both implementations and route through an application-level abstraction:

```java
public interface IndexStore {
    void save(Index index);
}

@Component
class ElasticsearchIndexStore implements IndexStore {
    // Use ElasticsearchOperations or an Elasticsearch repository
}

@Component
class RedisIndexStore implements IndexStore {
    // Use RedisTemplate or a Redis repository
}
```

A routing service can select the appropriate `IndexStore` for the current user.

Although a domain class can carry both `@Document` and `@RedisHash`, this may make Spring Data repository detection ambiguous when multiple modules are enabled. Keep repository interfaces in separately configured packages. For nontrivial applications, separate Elasticsearch and Redis persistence classes mapped from a shared domain model are often preferable because the two stores generally require different mappings and data layouts.

Final answer: A specific annotation cannot be disabled at runtime; conditionally configure the Spring Data repositories/beans or route through separate Redis and Elasticsearch implementations instead.

