A factorial is the product of all positive integers from `1` to `n`. By definition, `0! = 1`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

unsigned long long factorial(unsigned int n) {
    unsigned long long result = 1;

    for (unsigned int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
        result *= i;
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    std::cout << "Enter a non-negative integer (0-20): ";
    std::cin >> n;

    if (n < 0 || n > 20) {
        std::cout << "Input must be between 0 and 20.\n";
        return 1;
    }

    std::cout << n << "! = "
              << factorial(static_cast<unsigned int>(n)) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

`unsigned long long` can hold factorials only up to `20!`; larger factorials require an arbitrary-precision integer library such as Boost.Multiprecision.

Final answer: Use a loop starting with `result = 1` and multiply it by every integer from `2` through `n`; remember that `0! = 1`.

